**Public getter methods** in C++ are member functions that allow **access to private or protected class variables**. They provide a way to retrieve the value of a class's private data members from outside the class. These methods have no side effects and typically **return the value of the corresponding private variable**.

**Syntax:**
```cpp
// Getter method to retrieve the value of the private variable
dataType getVariableName() const { 
        return variableName; // Return the value of the private member 'variableName'
 }

// Accessing private member via getter method
objectName.getVariableName()
```

---

**Example:**
```cpp
#include  
using namespace std; 

class Car {
private:
    string color;  // Private variable: Stores car color

public:
    // Constructor initializes the car color when an object is created
    Car(string carColor) : color(carColor) {}

    // Public getter method: Retrieves the value of 'color'
    string getColor() const {
        return color;
    }
};

int main() {
    Car myCar("Red");  // Creating an object with a predefined color

    cout << "Car color: " << myCar.getColor() << endl;  // Displaying the car color using the getter

    return 0; 
}
```

**Expected Output:**
```default
Car color: Red
```

